{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-04-ratio-algebra-expressions",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Algebra with Ratios and Expressions",
    "description": "Use x:y as a substitution tool to simplify linear, quadratic, cubic, and reverse ratio expressions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach x=mk, y=nk as the main mental model. The common k cancels, so learners can evaluate complex-looking expressions without solving individual values. Later blocks reverse the process and include a quadratic relation with two possible ratios.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach the substitution habit x:y = m:n -> x=mk, y=nk. Use it to evaluate ratios of linear expressions, squares and cubes; simplify expressions by dividing through by y or y^2; and solve backwards when a ratio such as (2x+3y):(3x+5y) is given. Include reciprocal-expression patterns and a quadratic relation that can produce two possible ratios. Learners should avoid solving for x and y separately when only x:y is needed."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When x:y is already known, solving for x and y separately is often wasted work.\n\nWe’ll turn the ratio into simple multiples and use those multiples inside sums, differences, squares, cubes, and equations.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If x:y = 3:2, write x=3k and y=2k.\n\nNow any expression in x and y becomes an expression in one common k.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example:\n(2x+3y):(x+y)\n= (6k+6k):(3k+2k)\n= 12:5.\n\nThe k cancels because only the ratio matters.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
